## Profile-Specific Property Files

```
application.properties          (always loaded)
application-dev.properties      (loaded when 'dev' active)
application-prod.properties     (loaded when 'prod' active)
application-test.properties     (loaded when 'test' active)
```

Profile-specific files override base file values for the same key.

## @Profile on Beans

```java
@Service
@Profile("dev")
public class DevEmailService implements EmailService {
    public void send(String to, String subject, String body) {
        System.out.println("[DEV] Would send to: " + to);
    }
}

@Service
@Profile("prod")
public class ResendEmailService implements EmailService {
    public void send(String to, String subject, String body) {
        // Real Resend API call
    }
}
```

## @Profile on @Configuration Classes

```java
@Configuration
@Profile("prod")
public class ProdSecurityConfig {
    // Strict security settings for production
}

@Configuration
@Profile("dev")
public class DevSecurityConfig {
    // Relaxed CORS, no HTTPS enforcement
}
```

## Activating Profiles

```properties
# In application.properties
spring.profiles.active=dev

# Multiple profiles
spring.profiles.active=dev,featureX
```

```bash
# Environment variable
export SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod

# Command line
java -jar app.jar --spring.profiles.active=prod
```

## In Tests

```java
@SpringBootTest
@ActiveProfiles("test")
class ServiceTest { }
```

## Production Reality

A common mistake is having too many profiles. Keep it simple: `dev`, `test`, `prod` — sometimes `staging`. For feature flags, don't create a profile per feature; use regular boolean properties instead. Profiles are for environment-level concerns, not feature toggles.

## Implicit Default Profile

```java
// Active when NO profile is explicitly set
@Component
@Profile("default")
public class InMemoryUserStore implements UserStore {
    // Good for local dev without a database
}

// Active when 'prod' profile is set
@Component
@Profile("prod")
public class PostgresUserStore implements UserStore {
    // Real database implementation
}
```

## application-default.properties

```properties
# application-default.properties
# Loaded only when no profile is explicitly active
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:devdb
logging.level.root=DEBUG
```

## Changing the Default Profile

```properties
# application.properties
spring.profiles.default=local
```

Now when no profile is set, Spring acts as if `local` is active — loads `application-local.properties` and registers `@Profile("local")` beans.

## Checking Active Profiles

```java
@Autowired
private Environment environment;

public void logProfiles() {
    String[] profiles = environment.getActiveProfiles();
    // If empty: default profile is implicitly active
    System.out.println(Arrays.toString(profiles));
}
```

## Production Reality

Always ensure your production deployment explicitly sets `spring.profiles.active` or `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE`. Relying on the default profile in production is dangerous — a misconfigured deployment that forgets to set the profile silently runs with development configuration (H2 in-memory DB, disabled security, DEBUG logging).

## @ActiveProfiles

```java
@SpringBootTest
@ActiveProfiles("test")
class OrderServiceIntegrationTest {
    @Autowired
    private OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void shouldCreateOrder() {
        // Runs with application-test.properties loaded
    }
}
```

## application-test.properties

```properties
# Use H2 instead of PostgreSQL
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb;DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=org.h2.Driver
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop

# Disable security for simplicity
spring.security.enabled=false

# Stub external service URLs
app.payment-api.url=http://localhost:${wiremock.server.port}
```

## Per-Test Property Override

```java
@SpringBootTest(properties = {
    "spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop",
    "app.feature.enabled=true"
})
class FeatureFlagTest { }
```

## @TestPropertySource

```java
@SpringBootTest
@TestPropertySource(locations = "classpath:test.properties",
                   properties = "app.timeout=100")
class ServiceTest { }
```

## Production Reality

If your tests must run against a real database (testing database-specific features, constraints, stored procedures), use Testcontainers. It spins up a real PostgreSQL Docker container for the test session and tears it down afterward. This gives you test isolation without an always-on test database.

## @Profile on @Bean Methods

```java
@Configuration
public class ServiceConfig {

    @Bean
    @Profile("dev")
    public EmailService devEmailService() {
        return new LoggingEmailService(); // logs instead of sending
    }

    @Bean
    @Profile("prod")
    public EmailService prodEmailService() {
        return new SmtpEmailService(smtpConfig);
    }
}
```

## @Profile on @Configuration Classes

```java
@Configuration
@Profile("dev")
public class DevInfrastructureConfig {
    @Bean
    public DataSource embeddedDataSource() {
        return new EmbeddedDatabaseBuilder().setType(H2).build();
    }
    @Bean
    public H2ConsoleAutoConfiguration h2Console() {
        return new H2ConsoleAutoConfiguration();
    }
}
```

## Negation and Multi-Profile

```java
// Not production
@Bean
@Profile("!prod")
public MockPaymentGateway mockPayment() {
    return new MockPaymentGateway();
}

// Dev or staging
@Bean
@Profile({"dev", "staging"})
public DataGenerationService dataGenerator() {
    return new SeedDataService();
}
```

## @ConditionalOnProperty Alternative

```java
@Bean
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "app.cache.enabled", havingValue = "true")
public CacheManager redisCacheManager() {
    return new RedisCacheManager(redisConnectionFactory);
}
```

## Production Reality

Prefer `@ConditionalOnProperty` over `@Profile` when the condition is about a feature toggle rather than an environment. `@Profile` should answer "is this the production environment?" — `@ConditionalOnProperty` should answer "is this feature enabled?" Mixing the two purposes into `@Profile` creates confusion.

## Pattern: Interface + Multiple Implementations

```java
// Interface
public interface NotificationService {
    void send(String userId, String message);
}

// Dev implementation: log instead of send
@Service
@Profile("dev")
public class LoggingNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    public void send(String userId, String message) {
        log.info("[DEV] Notification to {}: {}", userId, message);
    }
}

// Production implementation: real push notification
@Service
@Profile("prod")
public class FirebaseNotificationService implements NotificationService {
    public void send(String userId, String message) {
        firebaseClient.send(userId, message);
    }
}
```

```java
// Consumer — no profile awareness needed
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final NotificationService notificationService;

    public OrderService(NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        processOrder(order);
        notificationService.send(order.getUserId(), "Order placed");
    }
}
```

## Anti-Pattern to Avoid

```java
// BAD: profile-checking in business logic
public void send(String userId, String message) {
    String[] profiles = environment.getActiveProfiles();
    if (Arrays.asList(profiles).contains("prod")) {
        firebaseClient.send(userId, message);
    } else {
        log.info("Would send: " + message);
    }
}
```

## @Configuration Variant

```java
@Configuration
public class StorageConfig {
    @Bean
    @Profile("prod")
    public StorageService s3Storage(S3Client client) {
        return new S3StorageService(client);
    }

    @Bean
    @Profile({"dev", "test"})
    public StorageService localStorage() {
        return new LocalFileStorageService("/tmp/dev-uploads");
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

Profile-based switching works well for infrastructure-level implementations. For business feature toggles — things that change independently of environment — use feature flags backed by properties or a feature flag service instead. Environment and features are separate concerns.
